so business is really heating up your  users are downloading the app  viewing your web pages clicking and  tapping is it ten times a second  thousand maybe it's a million you're  likely searching for a messaging service  to help you capture these events and  turn them into valuable features and  analysis  stay tuned because in today's episode we  are helping you evaluate the multiple  messaging options available  including pub sub to make your decision  easy  [Music]  all right so you are ready to make your  event data accessible  and useful be it for automated machine  learning based recommendations  or even just basic reporting building a  data pipeline  is what will enable a smooth flow of  your source device data  to your data warehouse or downstream  applications  the data pipeline automates ingesting  transforming  storing and routing data to its  destination  a pipeline looks something like this  with a front-end landing zone for  ingesting the data  a continuous processing system such as  data flow apache spark or one custom  built for transforming the data  and finally a data warehouse to store  and make the data available for  analytics  or for routing data to a downstream  application  in this video we are focusing on the  ingestion layer of data  pipeline and breaking down your options  so that you can make the best ingestion  service decision  for your use case when you collect data  from devices  each device will connect to an api  front-end  these front-ends might buffer data into  blobs  before moving them to a blob storage  service such as google cloud storage  or hadoop's hdfs buffering is critical  to the performance of these systems  but what if the idea of buffering a  large number of events on your front  ends  sounds risky or if you need that data in  real time  in this case you need a messaging  service to land your events  as they happen google cloud offers  messaging services that support  streaming data  pops up pub sub lite and confluent cloud  you can also run apache kafka yourself  the easiest way to handle messaging is  pub sub  as we've discussed in this series pub  sub is a service  built to make messaging just work  whether you send one message a day  or millions a second with pubsub you  create a topic  and publish messages to it from anywhere  in the world  anyone who needs to read these messages  creates a subscription  and sees all messages in one place no  matter where they originate  the readers and writers can scale  independently and have their own coders  so any team in your company that wants  to do  something with that data can just have  it and for those  writing processing applications pub sub  has one  important trick you can handle each  message independently allowing more  flexible parallelism  than you get with partition based  systems  overall this means that you simply don't  have to worry about  how your messages are ingested once you  teach your front end  to publish to pubs up of course it's not  just about ingestion  for large-scale stateful processing such  as pipelines that aggregate data into  sessions  or join multiple streams as well as  scalable connectors to databases such as  bigquery  and bigtable pub sub is integrated with  data flow  data flow is its own story but suffice  it to say  that you can create an auto-scaled  pipeline  pushing data to storage with a few  clicks  in the ui or write complex processing  logic  in java python sql and other languages  and have data flow run the code reliably  for you  there's another cool thing that puffs up  can do which is  supporting message delivery to web hooks  natively  so you can do lightweight but highly  scalable data processing and forwarding  in cloud functions app engine or a  custom app you run in gke  pubsub does a lot so it can be expensive  for some use cases  as an alternative pub sub lite provides  a cost effective service for real time  data streaming  working with pub sub lite has some  notable differences from working with  pubsa  first light has single zone data storage  rather than multi-zone as in the case of  pub sub  second light relies on partitions or  shards to achieve  parallelism and horizontal scaling this  is less flexible than pub subs model  of per message life cycle tracking and  third  with light you pay for the capacity  reserved for your partitions  whether you use it or not so there is  some  operational overhead to optimize  capacity planning  a bonus of light is that it will be  familiar to kafka users  in fact there's even a client library  that implements the kafka client api  this similarity also allows us to  provide an integration with apache spark  structured streaming  which is commonly used with kafka like  pubsub  light is integrated with data flow for  processing  it does not however offer push delivery  to web hooks  speaking of apache kafka no discussion  of real time streaming is complete  without it  so your options include running kafka  yourself  or using confluent cloud a managed  service  from the founders of kafka running kafka  yourself  is flexible and can be very cheap in  addition  kafka comes with a rich ecosystem of  tools that are simple to deploy  connectors to databases such as bigquery  but  operating kafka requires a substantial  commitment to administration  confluent provides all the benefits of  kafka ecosystem  with managed services and support when  you choose between kafka and pub sub it  is worth considering these factors  familiarity and existing software if you  already have experience  running kafka as well as applications  that rely on it  it may make sense to just stick with it  availability outside of google cloud if  you're planning to run a multi-cloud  system kafka may be a better choice  since it is available on virtually all  platforms  integration with google cloud now pub  sub is a first party service which means  a consistent authentication  administration  security features as well as consistent  support experience  access control when you have multiple  teams or companies that need to work  with the same data  pub subs resource level access control  and administration system  can be very helpful in addition to a  flexible sharing model  you also get independent quota and  billing even between readers and writers  to the same topic  in addition you don't have to think  about different clusters  of popping up there is only one  global pub sub service unpredictable  growth  now one of the advantages of pubsub is  that it does not make you commit to a  fixed capacity  be it cluster size or number of  partitions  this won't matter much if your workload  is steady  but if you are growing quickly or see  spikes in the workload  this model is very convenient so  what's the tldr when it comes to picking  a service to ingest  and land data use google cloud storage  when extremely low cost is high priority  and freshness is not the downside is  that you keep more data  on the front ends risking data loss and  complicated scaling  for real-time streaming options within  google cloud you have puff sub and pub  sub lite  puffs up is truly everything that you  need for most real-time cases  integrating seamlessly with other google  cloud services  pub sub aims for a no ops experience  with automatic scaling and pay for usage  rather than capacity  however because pubsub does a lot it's  not the cheapest messaging solution  when it comes to cost efficiency pub sub  lite delivers scalable  real time messaging at low cost  like pops up it has good support with  apache beam  and spark for processing and will be  more familiar to kafka users  it is however not entirely no ops  you will have to manage your own  capacity and there you have it the top  tools and characteristics to consider  when building out your data pipeline  hopefully we've made it easier to pick a  messaging service for your event driven  workload  which one of these messaging options are  you using share with me in the comments  below  and ask us questions on the pubs up  discuss list  thank you for joining us today on pubsub  made easy  [Music]  you
 

